1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,500
An original musical folktale made with AI
voices & AI music

2
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:05,950
♪ Make way, make way, for the King of the
Mountain!

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,850
♪ Bow down low to the best-dressed cat
around!

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,050
♪ (Hide your tails! Hide your ears!)

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,990
♪ (Eolssigu!)

6
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:22,110
♪ Who will go? Who will go?

7
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,150
♪ Not me, not me, oh no, no, no!

8
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:30,050
♪ (Up where the tigers are? Oh no!)

9
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:31,610
♪ Send the shrimp!

10
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:32,440
♪ (Too small!)

11
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:33,570
♪ Send the whale!

12
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:35,010
♪ (Too tall!)

13
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:36,410
♪ Send the eel!

14
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,410
♪ (Too slippery!)

15
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:40,700
♪ Send nobody at all!

16
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,550
♪ (Eolssigu!)

17
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:46,310
♪ Who will go? Who will go?

18
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:50,350
♪ Not me, not me, oh no, no, no!

19
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:52,680
♪ (Up where the tigers are? Oh no!)

20
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:54,710
♪ Who… will… go?

21
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,752
The bottom of the Southern Sea.

22
00:01:02,792 --> 00:01:08,433
A rabbit, tied with kelp, before the Dragon
King.

23
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:13,900
The cure for my sickness is inside that
rabbit.

24
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:17,961
Doctor, you may begin.

25
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:21,729
Hold still, little one.

26
00:01:21,769 --> 00:01:26,152
The King cannot wait.

27
00:01:26,672 --> 00:01:28,064
Steady.

28
00:01:28,284 --> 00:01:30,693
Look at me.

29
00:01:30,913 --> 00:01:35,434
You said we were friends.

30
00:01:36,134 --> 00:01:41,889
The terrapin who brought him here could not
say a single word.

31
00:01:42,229 --> 00:01:43,790
A knife.

32
00:01:43,029 --> 00:01:48,529
♪ Geomungo · 6-string zither (National Gugak
Center recording)

33
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:45,173
A king.

34
00:01:45,213 --> 00:01:48,852
A rabbit a mile under the sea.

35
00:01:48,892 --> 00:01:51,987
How does he ever get home?

36
00:01:53,497 --> 00:02:00,572
Twelve days earlier, that terrapin crawled
onto dry land for the first time.

37
00:02:01,092 --> 00:02:03,264
Well, hello there, stranger.

38
00:02:03,284 --> 00:02:04,460
Nice shell.

39
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:07,201
Are you lost, or just slow?

40
00:02:08,591 --> 00:02:13,991
♪ Hold on to my shell, hold on to my shell,

41
00:02:16,591 --> 00:02:21,991
♪ I won't let you fall, I won't let you
fall.

42
00:02:24,591 --> 00:02:27,091
♪ (Hold on, hold on…)

43
00:02:34,881 --> 00:02:37,207
Welcome back to the yard!

44
00:02:35,031 --> 00:02:42,506
[Culture note] Our host sings pansori,
Korea's one-singer opera. His tall black hat
is called a gat.

45
00:02:35,681 --> 00:02:41,181
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

46
00:02:37,227 --> 00:02:39,166
Pull up a straw mat.

47
00:02:39,186 --> 00:02:43,115
I sing, I tell, and I play every part.

48
00:02:43,305 --> 00:02:46,629
Tonight's tale is two hundred years old.

49
00:02:43,455 --> 00:02:50,820
[Culture note] This story is Sugungga, "Song
of the Underwater Palace," one of five
pansori epics.

50
00:02:46,649 --> 00:02:51,903
A king, a rabbit, and a very slow hero.

51
00:02:52,093 --> 00:02:56,473
And when a singer hits a good one, the whole
yard shouts back.

52
00:02:56,493 --> 00:02:58,116
Like this!

53
00:02:58,396 --> 00:03:06,256
[Culture note] Eolssigu! = "Yeah, that's
it!" Jota! = "Wonderful!" Real pansori
masters shout them tonight.

54
00:02:58,516 --> 00:03:00,692
That's it! Go on!

55
00:03:01,092 --> 00:03:02,843
Oh, come on.

56
00:03:02,863 --> 00:03:05,959
A seasick fish is louder than that!

57
00:03:06,359 --> 00:03:07,685
That's it!

58
00:03:07,705 --> 00:03:10,273
Go on! Sing it!

59
00:03:10,673 --> 00:03:17,148
Stay to the end, and I promise you three
answers.

60
00:03:18,598 --> 00:03:23,905
Why a mighty Dragon King needed a tiny
rabbit.

61
00:03:25,355 --> 00:03:31,565
How the slowest creature in the sea made a
tiger cry for his mother.

62
00:03:32,955 --> 00:03:39,036
And what one little rabbit said, with the
whole sea waiting to hear.

63
00:03:42,286 --> 00:03:45,608
Based on an old Korean folktale.

64
00:03:45,628 --> 00:03:48,980
Voices and songs were made with AI.

65
00:03:50,070 --> 00:03:58,940
Chapter 1: The King Who Ate Too Much
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'The Dragon King's
Feast'  ·  National Gugak Center
"He invited the Dragon Kings of the three
seas, seated lords and guests, and feasted
for days…"

66
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:06,761
Long ago, under the Southern Sea, stood a
palace of coral and blue tile.

67
00:04:06,951 --> 00:04:10,939
And on its throne sat the Dragon King.

68
00:04:07,101 --> 00:04:14,686
[Culture note] Koreans believed Dragon Kings
ruled the seas and sent rain. Fishermen
still honor them.

69
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:12,464
Mighty.

70
00:04:12,484 --> 00:04:17,371
Wise. And very, very hungry.

71
00:04:17,831 --> 00:04:20,593
More seaweed soup!

72
00:04:20,613 --> 00:04:22,861
More rice cakes!

73
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:25,784
More of everything!

74
00:04:26,184 --> 00:04:30,293
Majesty, that was your ninth bowl.

75
00:04:30,813 --> 00:04:33,473
Then this is the tenth.

76
00:04:33,493 --> 00:04:36,433
Count properly, Flounder.

77
00:04:36,833 --> 00:04:41,098
He had built a brand-new hall, so he threw a
feast.

78
00:04:41,118 --> 00:04:42,779
For one day.

79
00:04:42,799 --> 00:04:44,340
Then two.

80
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:45,936
Then forty.

81
00:04:46,216 --> 00:04:53,746
[Culture note] A Joseon king wore a red robe
with golden dragon circles. This one simply
is a dragon.

82
00:04:46,336 --> 00:04:51,497
Majesty, perhaps a little walk?

83
00:04:51,517 --> 00:04:56,033
Around the hall, just once?

84
00:04:56,493 --> 00:05:01,437
Walk? Kings don't walk, Doctor!

85
00:05:01,457 --> 00:05:06,345
Kings are carried!

86
00:05:06,805 --> 00:05:13,125
On the fortieth night, the Dragon King stood
up to dance.

87
00:05:13,145 --> 00:05:17,036
And fell flat on his royal face.

88
00:05:18,426 --> 00:05:21,791
Doctor Catfish felt the King's pulse.

89
00:05:18,576 --> 00:05:25,666
[Culture note] Royal doctors in Korea read
three pulses at each wrist to find what was
wrong.

90
00:05:21,831 --> 00:05:24,807
His long whiskers trembled.

91
00:05:25,327 --> 00:05:28,132
Hmm. Hmm-hmm.

92
00:05:28,132 --> 00:05:29,884
Hmmmm.

93
00:05:30,284 --> 00:05:33,879
Say something that isn't hmm!

94
00:05:34,279 --> 00:05:36,169
Majesty.

95
00:05:36,209 --> 00:05:47,132
Your pulse is slow, your belly is huge, and
your heart is heavy.

96
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:49,871
I knew all that!

97
00:05:49,891 --> 00:05:51,658
Cure it!

98
00:05:51,938 --> 00:05:59,138
[Culture note] In old Korean medicine, the
liver stored life energy. Hence a very
strange cure.

99
00:05:52,058 --> 00:06:01,423
The ancient book says only one thing will
cure you.

100
00:06:01,443 --> 00:06:07,204
The liver of a rabbit.

101
00:06:07,664 --> 00:06:09,371
A rabbit?

102
00:06:09,391 --> 00:06:12,554
What in the sea is a rabbit?

103
00:06:13,014 --> 00:06:18,294
A land animal, Majesty.

104
00:06:18,314 --> 00:06:20,348
Long ears.

105
00:06:20,368 --> 00:06:27,969
Lives high on the mountains, far above the
waves.

106
00:06:28,369 --> 00:06:29,892
Up there?

107
00:06:29,912 --> 00:06:34,171
Where the air is dry and the tigers are
hungry?

108
00:06:34,631 --> 00:06:37,940
I built a hall of pearl.

109
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:43,390
And now I cannot even sit up in it.

110
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:55,370
♪ I built a hall of coral with a roof of
ocean blue,

111
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:59,870
♪ I threw a feast for forty days and ate the
ocean too,

112
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:04,970
♪ The seaweed soup, the rice cakes piled
higher than the tide,

113
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:09,970
♪ And now there's thunder rumbling in my
royal inside!

114
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:15,390
♪ Oh, my royal belly! Oh, my royal head!

115
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:19,570
♪ The mighty Dragon King is stuck in his
royal bed!

116
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:22,870
♪ (Long live the King! Long live the King!)

117
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:28,400
♪ If the great old doctors came riding down
the sky,

118
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:34,170
♪ They'd feel my pulse and shake their heads
and tell me not to cry,

119
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:36,120
♪ (Hmm, hmm, hmm!)

120
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:38,900
♪ Your pulse is slow!

121
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:42,660
♪ Your belly's wide!

122
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:46,310
♪ Your heart is heavy, Majesty!

123
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:53,520
♪ (And your crown is on sideways!)

124
00:07:56,420 --> 00:08:00,530
♪ A king can rule the waters, a king can
rule the waves,

125
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:08,180
♪ But who can rule a tummy ache? Not even
kings are brave…

126
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:15,690
♪ Oh, my royal belly! Oh, my royal head!

127
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:19,770
♪ The mighty Dragon King is stuck in his
royal bed!

128
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:23,770
♪ (Long live the King! Long live the King!)

129
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:27,520
♪ Somebody… fetch me… something!

130
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:32,242
Call the whole court.

131
00:08:32,262 --> 00:08:36,588
Tomorrow, someone goes up to the land.

132
00:08:37,678 --> 00:08:45,588
Chapter 2: Who Will Go?
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'Who Will Save Me?'
·  National Gugak Center
The sick Dragon King cries out: "Who in all
the world will save my life?"

133
00:08:46,288 --> 00:08:51,434
The next morning, the whole court crowded
into the throne hall.

134
00:08:46,438 --> 00:08:53,803
[Culture note] Court officials wore badges
on the chest: cranes for scholars, tigers
for generals.

135
00:08:51,894 --> 00:08:55,137
My loyal ministers.

136
00:08:55,177 --> 00:09:00,109
Which of you will go up to the land and
bring me a rabbit?

137
00:09:00,629 --> 00:09:02,478
Nobody moved.

138
00:09:02,498 --> 00:09:05,495
Somewhere, a shrimp coughed.

139
00:09:05,895 --> 00:09:08,449
An excellent question, Majesty.

140
00:09:08,469 --> 00:09:11,670
I suggest the General.

141
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:15,254
Me? I'd love to!

142
00:09:15,274 --> 00:09:18,078
I'd go today!

143
00:09:18,098 --> 00:09:23,943
But, well, on land, humans catch octopus.

144
00:09:24,073 --> 00:09:28,114
They hang us on a line and dry us.

145
00:09:28,154 --> 00:09:31,778
For snacks.

146
00:09:32,028 --> 00:09:35,438
I suggest the Doctor.

147
00:09:35,838 --> 00:09:40,704
I am needed here, to say hmm.

148
00:09:44,234 --> 00:09:48,484
♪ I'd go myself this very day, I'd swim up
to the sand,

149
00:09:48,534 --> 00:09:54,884
♪ But both my eyes are on one side, I'd walk
in circles on the land!

150
00:09:54,934 --> 00:09:58,684
♪ Who will go? Who will go?

151
00:09:58,734 --> 00:10:03,084
♪ Not me, not me, oh no, no, no!

152
00:10:03,134 --> 00:10:06,724
♪ (Up where the tigers are? Oh no!)

153
00:10:08,134 --> 00:10:12,484
♪ I've got eight arms for fighting, and I'd
fight with every one,

154
00:10:12,534 --> 00:10:18,344
♪ But humans hang us on a line and dry us in
the sun!

155
00:10:19,434 --> 00:10:28,804
♪ I'd go, but I'm the doctor, and a doctor
has to stay,

156
00:10:29,334 --> 00:10:40,154
♪ Somebody must be here to frown and say
"hmm" every day!

157
00:10:43,534 --> 00:10:48,984
♪ We'd go, but we walk sideways, we would
never reach the shore,

158
00:10:49,034 --> 00:10:54,984
♪ We'd end up somewhere sideways and be lost
forevermore!

159
00:10:55,034 --> 00:10:58,784
♪ Who will go? Who will go?

160
00:10:58,834 --> 00:11:02,824
♪ Not me, not me, oh no, no, no!

161
00:11:03,134 --> 00:11:06,724
♪ (Up where the tigers are? Oh no!)

162
00:11:07,034 --> 00:11:08,284
♪ Send the shrimp!

163
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:09,114
♪ (Too small!)

164
00:11:09,134 --> 00:11:10,244
♪ Send the whale!

165
00:11:10,294 --> 00:11:11,684
♪ (Too tall!)

166
00:11:11,734 --> 00:11:13,084
♪ Send the eel!

167
00:11:13,134 --> 00:11:15,084
♪ (Too slippery!)

168
00:11:15,134 --> 00:11:17,374
♪ Send nobody at all!

169
00:11:17,424 --> 00:11:18,224
♪ (Eolssigu!)

170
00:11:19,134 --> 00:11:22,984
♪ Who will go? Who will go?

171
00:11:23,034 --> 00:11:27,024
♪ Not me, not me, oh no, no, no!

172
00:11:27,334 --> 00:11:29,354
♪ (Up where the tigers are? Oh no!)

173
00:11:29,404 --> 00:11:31,384
♪ Who… will… go?

174
00:11:34,274 --> 00:11:41,148
And then, from the very back of the hall, a
small, slow voice.

175
00:11:41,668 --> 00:11:43,709
I will go, Majesty.

176
00:11:44,109 --> 00:11:46,158
Who said that?

177
00:11:46,178 --> 00:11:48,952
I can't see anyone.

178
00:11:49,412 --> 00:11:51,578
Down here, Majesty.

179
00:11:51,598 --> 00:11:53,019
I'm on my way.

180
00:11:53,039 --> 00:11:55,037
I'll be there in a moment.

181
00:11:55,317 --> 00:12:02,847
[Culture note] Our terrapin is a Korean
soft-shell turtle: leathery shell, snorkel
nose, famous bite.

182
00:11:55,437 --> 00:11:57,054
The terrapin?

183
00:11:57,074 --> 00:12:00,313
He's the slowest creature in the sea!

184
00:12:00,773 --> 00:12:03,960
He looks after the royal inkstones.

185
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:06,616
Has anyone ever even noticed him?

186
00:12:07,016 --> 00:12:07,973
No, sir.

187
00:12:07,993 --> 00:12:10,319
But I can breathe air.

188
00:12:10,339 --> 00:12:15,414
My kind can walk on land and swim in the
sea.

189
00:12:15,814 --> 00:12:20,264
Why would you risk your little shell for me?

190
00:12:20,664 --> 00:12:24,715
Because once, Majesty, you saved it.

191
00:12:24,735 --> 00:12:27,118
You don't remember.

192
00:12:28,568 --> 00:12:33,871
Long ago, a baby terrapin got tangled in a
fisherman's net.

193
00:12:28,718 --> 00:12:35,863
[Culture note] Korean shadow plays were cast
on hanji, a strong paper made from mulberry
bark.

194
00:12:28,768 --> 00:12:34,268
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

195
00:12:34,061 --> 00:12:36,133
He kicked and kicked.

196
00:12:36,263 --> 00:12:40,622
Every fish swam past him.

197
00:12:40,752 --> 00:12:42,431
Nobody stopped.

198
00:12:42,771 --> 00:12:46,842
Then a young Dragon Prince swam by.

199
00:12:46,862 --> 00:12:50,565
He tore the net open with one claw.

200
00:12:51,025 --> 00:12:54,653
Go on, little one. Swim.

201
00:12:56,103 --> 00:12:59,170
Nobody else ever stopped for me.

202
00:12:59,190 --> 00:13:02,461
I've been yours ever since, Majesty.

203
00:13:02,921 --> 00:13:06,554
I'd forgotten that prince.

204
00:13:06,684 --> 00:13:10,381
What is your name, terrapin?

205
00:13:10,991 --> 00:13:13,269
Steady, Majesty.

206
00:13:13,289 --> 00:13:17,358
Mother said I was slow, but I always got
there.

207
00:13:17,638 --> 00:13:24,893
[Culture note] His old title, Byeoljubu,
means a minor court officer. We call him
Lord Terrapin.

208
00:13:17,758 --> 00:13:22,339
Then go, Steady, my Lord Terrapin.

209
00:13:22,359 --> 00:13:24,846
Bring me a rabbit.

210
00:13:25,246 --> 00:13:26,882
Yes, Majesty.

211
00:13:26,902 --> 00:13:28,865
Only, Majesty.

212
00:13:28,885 --> 00:13:31,881
What does a rabbit look like?

213
00:13:32,281 --> 00:13:34,455
Silence again.

214
00:13:34,475 --> 00:13:38,980
Nobody in the whole sea had ever seen one.

215
00:13:40,070 --> 00:13:50,120
Chapter 3: Draw Me a Rabbit
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'The Rabbit Portrait'
·  National Gugak Center
"Call the painter! Paint the portrait of the
rabbit!"

216
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:54,855
So they sent for the finest painter under
the sea.

217
00:13:50,970 --> 00:13:58,280
[Culture note] Joseon had a Royal Bureau of
Painting. Its painters drew kings, maps, and
animals.

218
00:13:54,875 --> 00:13:57,933
Master Inkwell, the squid.

219
00:13:58,393 --> 00:14:00,026
A rabbit?

220
00:14:00,046 --> 00:14:06,753
I have painted a thousand fish and one very
angry lobster.

221
00:14:06,773 --> 00:14:08,410
Describe it!

222
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:13,061
And I use my own ink.

223
00:14:13,081 --> 00:14:15,204
Very personal.

224
00:14:15,664 --> 00:14:22,099
Eyes like red jewels.

225
00:14:22,119 --> 00:14:29,631
Ears as long as bamboo leaves.

226
00:14:30,031 --> 00:14:31,414
And fins!

227
00:14:31,434 --> 00:14:33,588
Surely it has fins.

228
00:14:33,608 --> 00:14:35,966
Everything has fins.

229
00:14:36,366 --> 00:14:40,875
No fins, Minister.

230
00:14:40,895 --> 00:14:43,807
A nose that twitches.

231
00:14:43,827 --> 00:14:51,413
A tail like a ball of cotton.

232
00:14:51,813 --> 00:14:54,534
A cotton tail?

233
00:14:54,554 --> 00:14:57,991
Oh, this is going to be my masterpiece!

234
00:15:02,631 --> 00:15:04,581
♪ Bring me my brush! Bring me my stone!

235
00:15:04,631 --> 00:15:07,861
♪ Grind me the blackest ink I own!

236
00:15:08,981 --> 00:15:12,431
♪ Paint the eyes that gazed on the mountains
of the world,

237
00:15:12,481 --> 00:15:16,031
♪ Paint the mouth that nibbled orchids,
every petal curled,

238
00:15:16,081 --> 00:15:19,631
♪ Paint the ears that heard the cuckoo
calling in the rain,

239
00:15:19,681 --> 00:15:26,031
♪ Paint the nose that sniffed the mist on
the fairy mountain chain!

240
00:15:26,081 --> 00:15:28,831
♪ Draw me a rabbit, draw me a rabbit,

241
00:15:28,881 --> 00:15:32,731
♪ Dip in the ink and draw me a rabbit!

242
00:15:32,781 --> 00:15:35,481
♪ (Swish! Swish! Swish!)

243
00:15:35,781 --> 00:15:37,711
♪ Front feet short!

244
00:15:37,761 --> 00:15:38,931
♪ (Short!)

245
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:41,401
♪ Back feet long!

246
00:15:41,451 --> 00:15:42,491
♪ (Long!)

247
00:15:43,381 --> 00:15:45,051
♪ Ears stand up!

248
00:15:45,101 --> 00:15:45,901
♪ (Up!)

249
00:15:47,581 --> 00:15:50,461
♪ A tail like a puff of cotton!

250
00:15:50,511 --> 00:15:51,511
♪ (Puff!)

251
00:15:53,081 --> 00:15:56,931
♪ Paint the legs that bounded where the
spring flowers grow,

252
00:15:56,981 --> 00:16:01,431
♪ Paint the fur that kept him warm in the
winter snow,

253
00:16:01,481 --> 00:16:04,631
♪ Are you sure it has no fins?

254
00:16:04,681 --> 00:16:08,271
♪ Not one! Now hush, I'm painting!

255
00:16:08,581 --> 00:16:11,231
♪ Draw me a rabbit, draw me a rabbit,

256
00:16:11,281 --> 00:16:14,831
♪ Dip in the ink and draw me a rabbit!

257
00:16:14,881 --> 00:16:18,211
♪ (Swish! Swish! Swish!)

258
00:16:21,281 --> 00:16:24,731
♪ The half-moon rabbit on the mountain high

259
00:16:24,781 --> 00:16:28,821
♪ Was never as fine as this one, say I!

260
00:16:31,881 --> 00:16:34,531
♪ Draw me a rabbit, draw me a rabbit,

261
00:16:34,581 --> 00:16:37,131
♪ Dip in the ink and draw me a rabbit!

262
00:16:37,181 --> 00:16:39,241
♪ (Swish! Swish! Swish!)

263
00:16:39,781 --> 00:16:44,591
♪ Here you are, Lord Terrapin, take it and
go!

264
00:16:48,611 --> 00:16:50,593
And there it was.

265
00:16:48,761 --> 00:16:56,401
[Culture note] Korean folk paintings,
minhwa, gave animals big eyes, bright
colors, and a lot of charm.

266
00:16:50,613 --> 00:16:54,589
A perfect rabbit, in wet black ink.

267
00:16:55,049 --> 00:16:56,536
One rule.

268
00:16:56,556 --> 00:16:58,919
Do not get it wet.

269
00:16:59,319 --> 00:17:01,182
Master Inkwell.

270
00:17:01,202 --> 00:17:03,732
We live in the sea.

271
00:17:04,132 --> 00:17:07,237
Then don't get it very wet.

272
00:17:07,637 --> 00:17:13,159
Steady rolled up the portrait and tucked it
under the edge of his shell.

273
00:17:14,549 --> 00:17:18,338
At the palace gate, the court came to see
him off.

274
00:17:14,749 --> 00:17:20,249
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

275
00:17:18,358 --> 00:17:20,307
Well, some of it.

276
00:17:20,767 --> 00:17:22,528
Seaweed lunch.

277
00:17:22,548 --> 00:17:24,606
Seaweed snack.

278
00:17:24,626 --> 00:17:28,446
And a spare seaweed, in case.

279
00:17:28,906 --> 00:17:30,778
Listen, Terrapin.

280
00:17:30,798 --> 00:17:35,563
If you meet a tiger, just be bigger than the
tiger.

281
00:17:35,963 --> 00:17:38,873
I'm not bigger than anything, General.

282
00:17:39,273 --> 00:17:41,471
Then bite.

283
00:17:41,511 --> 00:17:45,685
Your kind bites hard.

284
00:17:45,725 --> 00:17:51,766
And once you bite, you never let go.

285
00:17:52,166 --> 00:17:59,201
[Culture note] An old saying: a soft-shell
turtle that bites won't let go until it
thunders.

286
00:17:52,286 --> 00:17:56,000
Bite. Never let go.

287
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:57,615
Yes, General.

288
00:17:58,015 --> 00:18:04,013
And the smallest officer in the Southern Sea
turned his nose up to the light.

289
00:18:05,223 --> 00:18:14,823
Chapter 4: Up Where the Dry Things Grow
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'High Above the
Shore'  ·  National Gugak Center
"High above the shore, the one red sun rises
over the eastern sea…"

290
00:18:15,523 --> 00:18:17,417
Up he swam.

291
00:18:15,673 --> 00:18:23,093
[Culture note] This journey's pansori verse
is "Gogocheonbyeon": high above the shore,
the red sun.

292
00:18:15,723 --> 00:18:21,223
♪ Gayageum sanjo · 12-string zither
(National Gugak Center recording)

293
00:18:17,437 --> 00:18:20,084
Past the kelp forests.

294
00:18:20,104 --> 00:18:23,207
Past the sleeping whales.

295
00:18:23,397 --> 00:18:26,918
Higher than he had ever been in his life.

296
00:18:27,378 --> 00:18:30,559
Steady, Steady.

297
00:18:30,599 --> 00:18:33,604
One flipper, then the other.

298
00:18:33,644 --> 00:18:35,968
That's all a journey is.

299
00:18:36,218 --> 00:18:39,699
I've never been higher than the kelp.

300
00:18:39,829 --> 00:18:44,216
What if the dry world doesn't want me?

301
00:18:58,256 --> 00:19:01,606
♪ Down in the blue where the lanterns sway,

302
00:19:01,656 --> 00:19:05,006
♪ I polish the inkstones every day,

303
00:19:05,056 --> 00:19:08,106
♪ Nobody sees me, nobody knows

304
00:19:08,156 --> 00:19:12,526
♪ The slowest shell in the palace rows.

305
00:19:12,856 --> 00:19:16,306
♪ But up past the kelp and up past the foam,

306
00:19:16,356 --> 00:19:19,306
♪ There's a world that's never been my home…

307
00:19:19,356 --> 00:19:21,806
♪ Up where the dry things grow,

308
00:19:21,856 --> 00:19:25,206
♪ Where the pine trees lean and the warm
winds blow,

309
00:19:25,256 --> 00:19:29,706
♪ I'll go, I'll go, though I'm small and
slow,

310
00:19:29,756 --> 00:19:33,346
♪ Up where the dry things grow!

311
00:19:42,056 --> 00:19:45,406
♪ Mother would say when the tide was high,

312
00:19:45,456 --> 00:19:48,206
♪ "The fish can't see it, but there's a sky,

313
00:19:48,256 --> 00:19:51,006
♪ A sky so wide that it has no floor,

314
00:19:51,056 --> 00:19:54,006
♪ And a sun as warm as an open door."

315
00:19:54,056 --> 00:19:57,506
♪ Steady, steady, one flipper at a time,

316
00:19:57,556 --> 00:20:02,036
♪ Slow as the tide, but I'll get there fine.

317
00:20:05,056 --> 00:20:08,406
♪ The King who saved me long ago

318
00:20:08,456 --> 00:20:11,406
♪ Needs one small terrapin now, and so…

319
00:20:11,456 --> 00:20:14,926
♪ Up where the dry things grow,

320
00:20:16,856 --> 00:20:20,206
♪ Where the pine trees lean and the warm
winds blow,

321
00:20:20,256 --> 00:20:24,706
♪ I'll go, I'll go, though I'm small and
slow,

322
00:20:24,756 --> 00:20:28,346
♪ Up where the dry things grow!

323
00:20:38,856 --> 00:20:41,906
♪ Steady, steady, one flipper at a time,

324
00:20:41,956 --> 00:20:46,436
♪ Slow as the tide, but I'll get there fine.

325
00:20:48,366 --> 00:20:52,772
And then his nose broke the surface.

326
00:20:48,516 --> 00:20:55,606
[Culture note] Korea's rocky coasts are
lined with black pines, bent into shapes by
the wind.

327
00:20:48,566 --> 00:20:54,066
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

328
00:20:52,792 --> 00:20:55,149
Sun. Wind.

329
00:20:55,169 --> 00:20:58,203
Pine trees on red rocks.

330
00:20:58,663 --> 00:21:01,184
So this is dry.

331
00:21:01,204 --> 00:21:03,326
It's very dry.

332
00:21:03,346 --> 00:21:05,378
It's dry all the way up!

333
00:21:05,568 --> 00:21:09,164
Fish! Fish swimming in the air!

334
00:21:09,184 --> 00:21:10,761
No. Birds.

335
00:21:10,781 --> 00:21:13,614
Mother said they're called birds.

336
00:21:14,014 --> 00:21:17,217
A seagull looked at Steady.

337
00:21:17,237 --> 00:21:20,438
Steady bowed to the seagull.

338
00:21:20,458 --> 00:21:22,418
The seagull left.

339
00:21:22,818 --> 00:21:24,031
Thank you.

340
00:21:24,051 --> 00:21:25,872
Lovely to meet you.

341
00:21:25,892 --> 00:21:28,349
I'm from the sea.

342
00:21:28,749 --> 00:21:31,470
So he started up the mountain.

343
00:21:31,490 --> 00:21:34,169
One flipper, then the other.

344
00:21:34,359 --> 00:21:38,932
By noon he had passed three pine trees.

345
00:21:38,952 --> 00:21:41,673
By sunset, a fourth.

346
00:21:43,063 --> 00:21:46,624
The next day he reached the high meadows.

347
00:21:43,213 --> 00:21:50,743
[Culture note] Pink azaleas cover Korea's
hills every April. People climb mountains
just to see them.

348
00:21:46,644 --> 00:21:49,912
Pink azaleas on every slope.

349
00:21:49,932 --> 00:21:53,179
A stream hopping over stones.

350
00:21:53,639 --> 00:21:55,275
Pink flowers!

351
00:21:55,295 --> 00:21:56,937
On the ground!

352
00:21:56,957 --> 00:22:00,346
Nobody at the palace will believe this.

353
00:22:00,746 --> 00:22:03,827
He took out the portrait to check.

354
00:22:03,847 --> 00:22:06,151
It felt a little damp.

355
00:22:06,171 --> 00:22:09,027
He did not look closely.

356
00:22:09,047 --> 00:22:10,297
He should have.

357
00:22:10,697 --> 00:22:12,056
Long ears.

358
00:22:12,076 --> 00:22:14,622
A cotton tail.

359
00:22:14,642 --> 00:22:19,563
Somewhere up here there's a rabbit, and I
will find him.

360
00:22:19,963 --> 00:22:23,330
Then he stopped walking.

361
00:22:23,460 --> 00:22:28,046
Because he remembered what would happen when
he did.

362
00:22:28,656 --> 00:22:33,545
And then I'll take him home to the King.

363
00:22:34,845 --> 00:22:43,455
Chapter 5: The Seat of Honor
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'The Rabbit Brags'  ·
National Gugak Center
The rabbit boasts: "The immortals of the
mountains? I took them on as my students!"

364
00:22:44,155 --> 00:22:47,867
Meanwhile, high on a hill covered in clover,

365
00:22:47,867 --> 00:22:51,000
the animals held a spring picnic.

366
00:22:51,190 --> 00:22:55,162
There was one big flat rock under the old
shade tree.

367
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:58,485
[Culture note] The eldest gets the seat of
honor. That's why Koreans often ask your age
first.

368
00:22:55,182 --> 00:22:57,545
The seat of honor.

369
00:22:57,565 --> 00:22:59,511
Three animals wanted it.

370
00:22:59,911 --> 00:23:02,629
I am the eldest.

371
00:23:02,649 --> 00:23:07,038
My antlers are older than that big old tree.

372
00:23:07,438 --> 00:23:10,445
You don't have antlers, dear.

373
00:23:10,845 --> 00:23:14,406
My husband's antlers, then.

374
00:23:14,426 --> 00:23:16,330
Same thing.

375
00:23:16,730 --> 00:23:21,399
Pah! I dug the very first hole on this
mountain.

376
00:23:21,419 --> 00:23:24,889
I'm the eldest!

377
00:23:25,289 --> 00:23:33,388
I remember when this mountain was a little
hill.

378
00:23:33,408 --> 00:23:35,874
I'm the eldest.

379
00:23:49,164 --> 00:23:52,314
♪ Who's the oldest? Who's the oldest?

380
00:23:52,364 --> 00:23:56,414
♪ Who gets the seat by the old shade tree?

381
00:23:56,464 --> 00:24:00,314
♪ (Not you! Not you! That seat's for me!)

382
00:24:00,364 --> 00:24:03,414
♪ When I was a fawn, the moon was new,

383
00:24:03,464 --> 00:24:07,114
♪ The rivers were only a drop of dew,

384
00:24:07,164 --> 00:24:11,424
♪ I taught the very first rain to fall,

385
00:24:11,804 --> 00:24:14,114
♪ (Oh, here she goes again, y'all!)

386
00:24:14,164 --> 00:24:17,414
♪ I dug the valleys with my snout,

387
00:24:17,464 --> 00:24:20,414
♪ I pushed the mountains in and out,

388
00:24:20,464 --> 00:24:22,614
♪ Before me there was only mud,

389
00:24:22,664 --> 00:24:25,614
♪ (And look at you, still covered, bud!)

390
00:24:25,664 --> 00:24:29,214
♪ Who's the oldest? Who's the oldest?

391
00:24:29,264 --> 00:24:33,014
♪ Who gets the seat by the old shade tree?

392
00:24:33,064 --> 00:24:37,954
♪ (Not you! Not you! That seat's for me!)

393
00:24:38,064 --> 00:24:41,374
♪ I sat right here when this great hill

394
00:24:41,864 --> 00:24:44,814
♪ Was just a pebble, small and still,

395
00:24:44,864 --> 00:24:48,904
♪ I watched it grow up whisker-high,

396
00:24:50,464 --> 00:24:55,154
♪ (He slept right through it! Oh my, oh my!)

397
00:24:56,464 --> 00:24:58,114
♪ I'm older!

398
00:24:58,164 --> 00:25:00,174
♪ No, I'm older!

399
00:25:03,084 --> 00:25:04,414
♪ I'm the oldest!

400
00:25:04,464 --> 00:25:07,384
♪ (Now who's the boldest?)

401
00:25:07,964 --> 00:25:09,864
♪ (Eolssigu!)

402
00:25:10,564 --> 00:25:13,614
♪ Who's the oldest? Who's the oldest?

403
00:25:13,664 --> 00:25:17,414
♪ Who gets the seat by the old shade tree?

404
00:25:17,464 --> 00:25:22,154
♪ (Not you! Not you! That seat's for me!)

405
00:25:24,694 --> 00:25:26,630
Oh, dear.

406
00:25:26,650 --> 00:25:28,726
Oh, dear, dear.

407
00:25:28,746 --> 00:25:30,629
You're all babies.

408
00:25:30,759 --> 00:25:33,860
When they hung the sky, who held the ladder?

409
00:25:33,880 --> 00:25:37,386
Me. Who handed up the stars? Me.

410
00:25:37,786 --> 00:25:40,193
That's a lie.

411
00:25:40,713 --> 00:25:42,422
Prove it.

412
00:25:42,882 --> 00:25:44,042
They couldn't.

413
00:25:44,062 --> 00:25:48,524
So Clover the rabbit sat down on the seat of
honor.

414
00:25:48,544 --> 00:25:50,443
As usual.

415
00:25:51,833 --> 00:25:56,977
Just then, puffing and very slow, a stranger
arrived.

416
00:25:56,997 --> 00:26:00,171
He had been climbing for a day and a half.

417
00:26:00,571 --> 00:26:02,052
Excuse me.

418
00:26:02,072 --> 00:26:03,402
Good morning.

419
00:26:03,422 --> 00:26:07,503
Has anyone here seen this creature?

420
00:26:07,903 --> 00:26:11,049
But the sea had soaked the painting.

421
00:26:11,089 --> 00:26:14,536
The ears had run into stripes.

422
00:26:14,576 --> 00:26:18,317
The nose had run into fangs.

423
00:26:18,837 --> 00:26:20,581
It's him!

424
00:26:20,601 --> 00:26:23,313
Stripes and fangs!

425
00:26:23,773 --> 00:26:25,597
That's the tiger!

426
00:26:25,667 --> 00:26:28,945
He's looking for the tiger!

427
00:26:29,495 --> 00:26:30,808
Tiger?

428
00:26:30,808 --> 00:26:32,650
No, no.

429
00:26:32,670 --> 00:26:33,857
It's a rab-

430
00:26:34,797 --> 00:26:39,790
And from high up the valley came a voice
like a landslide.

431
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:51,680
Chapter 6: The Tiger Comes Down
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'The Tiger Comes
Down'  ·  National Gugak Center
"The tiger comes down, the tiger comes down,
down from the deep pine valley, a great
beast!"

432
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:54,536
Down the valley he came.

433
00:26:52,530 --> 00:26:59,785
[Culture note] In the old pansori, the tiger
comes down "so softly not a single ant is
crushed."

434
00:26:54,576 --> 00:26:56,947
Soft paws.

435
00:26:56,987 --> 00:26:59,093
Slow steps.

436
00:26:59,133 --> 00:27:02,334
Not one ant crushed.

437
00:27:02,854 --> 00:27:06,457
who is looking for me?

438
00:27:07,997 --> 00:27:10,165
Big Stripes.

439
00:27:08,147 --> 00:27:15,237
[Culture note] Korean folk painters drew
tigers big-eyed and goofy, often teased by a
magpie.

440
00:27:10,185 --> 00:27:12,766
The King of the Mountain.

441
00:27:12,786 --> 00:27:14,742
In his own opinion.

442
00:27:15,142 --> 00:27:18,583
Somebody has a portrait of me?

443
00:27:18,603 --> 00:27:21,286
Oh, it's a good likeness.

444
00:27:21,306 --> 00:27:22,989
A little wet.

445
00:27:29,019 --> 00:27:32,279
♪ (Shh! Shh! Something's in the pines!)

446
00:27:35,119 --> 00:27:39,369
♪ Claws like iron sickles, and a tail as
long as a pole,

447
00:27:39,419 --> 00:27:44,169
♪ Stripes like a painted screen from my
whiskers to my soul,

448
00:27:44,219 --> 00:27:49,169
♪ My front legs are like pillars and my back
legs like a drum,

449
00:27:49,219 --> 00:27:54,809
♪ I tiptoe down so softly not an ant knows I
have come!

450
00:27:55,819 --> 00:28:00,669
♪ Make way, make way, for the King of the
Mountain!

451
00:28:00,719 --> 00:28:04,569
♪ Bow down low to the best-dressed cat
around!

452
00:28:04,619 --> 00:28:07,769
♪ (Hide your tails! Hide your ears!)

453
00:28:07,819 --> 00:28:09,709
♪ (Eolssigu!)

454
00:28:16,219 --> 00:28:20,969
♪ The deer all faint, the boars all run, the
rabbits hide their ears,

455
00:28:21,019 --> 00:28:25,469
♪ The magpies laugh behind my back, but who
cares about their jeers?

456
00:28:25,519 --> 00:28:30,239
♪ I roar one roar, the pine trees bow, the
rivers change their course,

457
00:28:30,289 --> 00:28:31,769
♪ (Has he ever tried it?)

458
00:28:31,819 --> 00:28:35,189
♪ Well, not yet. But of course!

459
00:28:35,319 --> 00:28:40,239
♪ Look at me, look at me, handsome as a
painting,

460
00:28:40,619 --> 00:28:44,879
♪ Somebody fan me, I think I'm fainting…

461
00:28:46,219 --> 00:28:51,069
♪ Make way, make way, for the King of the
Mountain!

462
00:28:51,119 --> 00:28:54,869
♪ Bow down low to the best-dressed cat
around!

463
00:28:54,919 --> 00:28:58,269
♪ (Hide your tails! Hide your ears!)

464
00:28:58,319 --> 00:29:00,209
♪ (Eolssigu!)

465
00:29:03,009 --> 00:29:09,439
♪ (Jota!)

466
00:29:14,359 --> 00:29:16,679
And what are you?

467
00:29:16,699 --> 00:29:19,864
A rock with a head?

468
00:29:19,884 --> 00:29:23,021
A dumpling with legs?

469
00:29:23,421 --> 00:29:29,240
I am Lord Terrapin, of the Dragon King's
court.

470
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:35,335
Never heard of him.

471
00:29:35,355 --> 00:29:37,882
But I'm hungry.

472
00:29:37,902 --> 00:29:42,138
Terrapin soup!

473
00:29:42,598 --> 00:29:46,044
Steady pulled his head into his shell.

474
00:29:46,084 --> 00:29:48,025
Everything went dark.

475
00:29:48,545 --> 00:29:51,155
Bite. Never let go.

476
00:29:51,175 --> 00:29:53,825
Bite. Never let go.

477
00:29:54,285 --> 00:29:57,692
Hello? Anybody in there?

478
00:29:57,712 --> 00:30:00,833
Come out, little dumpling.

479
00:30:01,633 --> 00:30:05,394
snap! Right on the nose!

480
00:30:05,944 --> 00:30:10,705
ow! ow ow ow!

481
00:30:10,775 --> 00:30:12,789
Let go!

482
00:30:12,859 --> 00:30:14,872
Let go!

483
00:30:15,422 --> 00:30:17,622
"Mm-mm," said Steady.

484
00:30:17,642 --> 00:30:19,946
He did not let go.

485
00:30:20,346 --> 00:30:23,857
who are you?

486
00:30:24,557 --> 00:30:29,218
"Mm the Dragon King's tiger-biting general.

487
00:30:29,238 --> 00:30:32,085
Mm have fifty brothers."

488
00:30:32,485 --> 00:30:33,958
mother!

489
00:30:34,658 --> 00:30:39,249
And the King of the Mountain ran all the way
home.

490
00:30:39,269 --> 00:30:43,213
Steady fell on his back, legs waving.

491
00:30:44,603 --> 00:30:46,905
You bit a tiger.

492
00:30:44,803 --> 00:30:50,303
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

493
00:30:46,925 --> 00:30:48,145
On the nose.

494
00:30:48,165 --> 00:30:51,082
I saw it with both eyes!

495
00:30:51,542 --> 00:30:53,038
Is he gone?

496
00:30:53,058 --> 00:30:55,409
I can't see.

497
00:30:55,429 --> 00:30:57,731
I'm upside down.

498
00:30:58,131 --> 00:31:02,906
Clover pushed with all his might, and
flipped him over.

499
00:31:03,306 --> 00:31:09,397
Friend, you are the bravest rock with a head
I ever met.

500
00:31:09,857 --> 00:31:12,498
Long ears.

501
00:31:12,518 --> 00:31:14,808
Red eyes.

502
00:31:14,828 --> 00:31:17,942
A tail like cotton.

503
00:31:17,962 --> 00:31:21,159
You're a rabbit.

504
00:31:21,619 --> 00:31:23,401
Last time I looked.

505
00:31:23,801 --> 00:31:25,074
I found him.

506
00:31:26,224 --> 00:31:36,764
Chapter 7: The Palace Under the Sea
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'Our Palace Under the
Sea'  ·  National Gugak Center
The terrapin tempts the rabbit: "Our palace
under the sea is a world apart!"

507
00:31:37,464 --> 00:31:41,665
That evening, the two of them sat together
on the seat of honor.

508
00:31:37,664 --> 00:31:43,164
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

509
00:31:41,685 --> 00:31:44,006
It was big enough for two.

510
00:31:44,466 --> 00:31:49,105
You told them you held the ladder when they
hung the sky.

511
00:31:49,125 --> 00:31:51,169
Was that true?

512
00:31:51,569 --> 00:31:53,931
Of course not!

513
00:31:53,951 --> 00:31:58,104
Rabbits talk fast, so nobody notices we're
scared.

514
00:31:58,294 --> 00:31:59,877
Hawks up there.

515
00:32:00,007 --> 00:32:03,793
Foxes down here.

516
00:32:03,923 --> 00:32:07,428
Hunters with dogs every autumn.

517
00:32:07,768 --> 00:32:11,562
I've never slept a whole night in my life.

518
00:32:11,692 --> 00:32:13,656
One ear is always up.

519
00:32:14,146 --> 00:32:21,566
[Culture note] Koreans see a rabbit in the
full moon, pounding rice cakes with a mortar
and pestle.

520
00:32:14,266 --> 00:32:16,722
That sounds lonely.

521
00:32:17,182 --> 00:32:18,295
Lonely?

522
00:32:18,315 --> 00:32:22,153
Me? I'm the eldest on the mountain!

523
00:32:22,173 --> 00:32:24,039
Everybody knows me!

524
00:32:24,169 --> 00:32:25,551
They know me.

525
00:32:25,681 --> 00:32:29,899
They just run away with me, or after me.

526
00:32:30,029 --> 00:32:32,178
Nobody ever stays.

527
00:32:42,918 --> 00:32:46,968
♪ Hawk in the morning, fox in the night,

528
00:32:47,018 --> 00:32:50,568
♪ Hunters at harvest with their dogs in
sight,

529
00:32:50,618 --> 00:32:54,168
♪ I talk so fast and I jump so high,

530
00:32:54,218 --> 00:32:57,868
♪ Nobody sees the fear in my eye.

531
00:32:57,918 --> 00:33:01,568
♪ Always running, always running,

532
00:33:01,618 --> 00:33:05,068
♪ One ear up and one foot on the ground,

533
00:33:05,118 --> 00:33:09,048
♪ Always running, always running,

534
00:33:09,118 --> 00:33:13,048
♪ Never once have I turned around.

535
00:33:15,518 --> 00:33:19,068
♪ They say I'm the oldest, the cleverest
one,

536
00:33:19,118 --> 00:33:22,768
♪ The rabbit who helped hang the moon and
sun,

537
00:33:22,818 --> 00:33:26,368
♪ But who is there when the night comes
down?

538
00:33:26,418 --> 00:33:30,898
♪ Who stays when the danger's all around?

539
00:33:33,718 --> 00:33:37,468
♪ Four little leaves on a clover hill,

540
00:33:37,518 --> 00:33:41,448
♪ Happy where my feet stand still.

541
00:33:44,418 --> 00:33:48,168
♪ Just once I'd like to close both eyes

542
00:33:48,218 --> 00:33:51,368
♪ And sleep until the sun can rise,

543
00:33:51,418 --> 00:33:55,168
♪ With someone near who won't run away,

544
00:33:55,218 --> 00:33:58,868
♪ Someone who'll stay… someone who'll stay.

545
00:33:58,918 --> 00:34:02,668
♪ Always running, always running,

546
00:34:02,718 --> 00:34:06,168
♪ One ear up and one foot on the ground,

547
00:34:06,218 --> 00:34:09,768
♪ Always running, always running,

548
00:34:09,818 --> 00:34:13,168
♪ Never once have I turned around.

549
00:34:13,218 --> 00:34:17,068
♪ Four little leaves on a clover hill,

550
00:34:17,118 --> 00:34:21,048
♪ Happy where my feet stand still.

551
00:34:24,918 --> 00:34:27,508
♪ Someday… someday.

552
00:34:36,138 --> 00:34:40,357
Steady looked at this funny, frightened
rabbit.

553
00:34:40,397 --> 00:34:43,813
And then he thought of his sick king.

554
00:34:44,333 --> 00:34:45,572
Forgive me.

555
00:34:47,022 --> 00:34:50,996
Where I come from, there are no hawks.

556
00:34:51,016 --> 00:34:52,704
No foxes.

557
00:34:52,724 --> 00:34:54,595
No hunters.

558
00:34:54,995 --> 00:34:58,446
None? Not even one tiny hawk?

559
00:34:58,906 --> 00:35:00,556
Not one.

560
00:35:00,576 --> 00:35:04,912
And the Dragon King himself wants to meet
you.

561
00:35:04,932 --> 00:35:06,564
Very much.

562
00:35:06,964 --> 00:35:09,053
Every word of it was true.

563
00:35:09,073 --> 00:35:11,916
That was the terrible part.

564
00:35:12,376 --> 00:35:14,592
A king wants to meet me?

565
00:35:26,122 --> 00:35:31,972
♪ There's a palace under the sea with a roof
of tiles so blue,

566
00:35:32,022 --> 00:35:37,372
♪ And lanterns made of pearl that glow the
whole night through,

567
00:35:37,422 --> 00:35:38,472
♪ Are there hawks?

568
00:35:38,522 --> 00:35:39,672
♪ Not a single one.

569
00:35:39,722 --> 00:35:40,772
♪ And foxes?

570
00:35:40,822 --> 00:35:42,172
♪ Not a one.

571
00:35:42,222 --> 00:35:48,372
♪ The palace under the sea, the palace under
the sea,

572
00:35:48,422 --> 00:35:51,372
♪ Where nobody chases anybody,

573
00:35:51,422 --> 00:35:54,372
♪ The palace under the sea!

574
00:35:54,422 --> 00:35:59,072
♪ The King himself would like to meet you at
his door,

575
00:35:59,122 --> 00:36:04,122
♪ He wants to meet you, Clover, more than
anyone before,

576
00:36:04,222 --> 00:36:06,722
♪ Me? A rabbit with a crooked ear?

577
00:36:06,722 --> 00:36:09,122
♪ The King of all the sea wants me down
there?

578
00:36:09,222 --> 00:36:12,022
♪ A seat of honor, a pillow of silk,

579
00:36:12,222 --> 00:36:14,872
♪ Seaweed cakes and a cup of milk,

580
00:36:14,922 --> 00:36:20,222
♪ And sleep, a whole night through, with
both my ears laid down!

581
00:36:24,722 --> 00:36:27,322
♪ Every word I say is true,

582
00:36:29,222 --> 00:36:32,722
♪ Then why does my heart feel heavy too?

583
00:36:37,522 --> 00:36:43,672
♪ The palace under the sea, the palace under
the sea,

584
00:36:43,722 --> 00:36:46,772
♪ Where nobody chases anybody,

585
00:36:46,822 --> 00:36:50,082
♪ The palace under the sea!

586
00:36:52,592 --> 00:36:55,040
Wait. What's the catch?

587
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:57,121
There's always a catch.

588
00:36:57,641 --> 00:37:01,082
Steady opened his mouth.

589
00:37:01,122 --> 00:37:05,506
He had never told a lie in his life.

590
00:37:06,026 --> 00:37:10,807
The King needs a very important guest.

591
00:37:10,847 --> 00:37:15,263
You would be the most important one.

592
00:37:15,783 --> 00:37:16,870
Important.

593
00:37:16,890 --> 00:37:20,306
Me. A rabbit could get used to that.

594
00:37:20,436 --> 00:37:23,357
There's just one thing.

595
00:37:23,397 --> 00:37:25,115
I can't swim.

596
00:37:25,635 --> 00:37:27,696
Hold on to my shell.

597
00:37:27,716 --> 00:37:29,788
I won't let you fall.

598
00:37:30,938 --> 00:37:41,688
Chapter 8: Hold On to My Shell
♪ Pansori · Simcheongga — 'The Wide, Wide
Sea'  ·  National Gugak Center
From another pansori, a sea voyage: "The
wide, wide sea, the rolling waves…"

599
00:37:42,388 --> 00:37:48,084
At sunrise, a rabbit rode out to sea on the
back of a terrapin.

600
00:37:42,538 --> 00:37:49,903
[Culture note] Old Korean paintings drew the
sea as neat curling waves, like the scales
of a fish.

601
00:37:48,104 --> 00:37:50,021
Screaming.

602
00:37:50,151 --> 00:37:53,881
Clover squealed at every single wave.

603
00:37:53,901 --> 00:37:59,908
Steady calmly explained that wet was the
main thing about the sea.

604
00:38:00,038 --> 00:38:07,279
A big wave lifted them up, and dropped them
down, and lifted them up again.

605
00:38:08,669 --> 00:38:14,710
Halfway across, they rested on a little rock
with one bent pine tree.

606
00:38:08,869 --> 00:38:14,369
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

607
00:38:15,170 --> 00:38:21,072
That was wonderful!

608
00:38:21,092 --> 00:38:24,330
The going up, and the coming down!

609
00:38:24,350 --> 00:38:26,616
Do it again!

610
00:38:26,806 --> 00:38:30,067
Steady, have you ever laughed?

611
00:38:30,087 --> 00:38:31,735
Even once?

612
00:38:31,755 --> 00:38:32,618
Try it.

613
00:38:32,638 --> 00:38:34,729
Say whee.

614
00:38:35,129 --> 00:38:36,750
Whee.

615
00:38:37,150 --> 00:38:38,890
With your heart!

616
00:38:39,350 --> 00:38:41,833
Whee! Ha!

617
00:38:41,853 --> 00:38:45,241
Ha ha ha!

618
00:38:45,701 --> 00:38:50,863
Nobody in the Southern Sea had ever heard
the terrapin laugh.

619
00:38:50,883 --> 00:38:53,156
Not even the terrapin.

620
00:38:53,616 --> 00:38:57,218
You know, you're the first one who ever
carried me.

621
00:38:57,238 --> 00:38:59,502
Everyone else just chases me.

622
00:38:59,962 --> 00:39:04,276
Steady's heart turned over inside his shell.

623
00:39:04,796 --> 00:39:06,037
Clover.

624
00:39:06,077 --> 00:39:08,957
There's something I have to tell you.

625
00:39:08,997 --> 00:39:12,587
About the King. He is-

626
00:39:13,107 --> 00:39:15,055
Look! A whale!

627
00:39:15,075 --> 00:39:16,759
He's waving at us!

628
00:39:16,779 --> 00:39:18,374
Hello, whale!

629
00:39:18,834 --> 00:39:21,279
And the moment was gone.

630
00:39:35,379 --> 00:39:38,929
♪ The waves go up, the waves go down,

631
00:39:38,979 --> 00:39:42,429
♪ The gulls fly high, the sun's a crown,

632
00:39:42,479 --> 00:39:45,829
♪ I've never been this far from home,

633
00:39:45,879 --> 00:39:48,529
♪ With nothing below but blue and foam.

634
00:39:48,579 --> 00:39:54,229
♪ Hold on to my shell, hold on to my shell,

635
00:39:54,279 --> 00:39:59,179
♪ I won't let you fall, I won't let you
fall.

636
00:40:01,179 --> 00:40:04,829
♪ I'm not very fast and I'm not very tall,

637
00:40:04,879 --> 00:40:08,129
♪ I'm hardly a hero at all, at all,

638
00:40:08,179 --> 00:40:11,029
♪ But I've got a back that is broad and
strong,

639
00:40:11,079 --> 00:40:14,629
♪ And a heart that can carry you all night
long.

640
00:40:14,679 --> 00:40:17,529
♪ Everyone else just chased me down,

641
00:40:17,579 --> 00:40:20,829
♪ The fox, the hawk, the hounds from town,

642
00:40:20,879 --> 00:40:24,029
♪ You're the first who ever carried me,

643
00:40:24,079 --> 00:40:28,449
♪ Who knew a friend was a rock in the sea?

644
00:40:35,779 --> 00:40:40,149
♪ Oh, if he knew where this road will end,

645
00:40:42,879 --> 00:40:46,919
♪ Would he still call me his friend?

646
00:40:48,479 --> 00:40:54,129
♪ Hold on to my shell, hold on to my shell,

647
00:40:54,179 --> 00:40:59,079
♪ I won't let you fall, I won't let you
fall.

648
00:41:00,179 --> 00:41:01,559
♪ Whee!

649
00:41:09,109 --> 00:41:12,231
Then Steady dived.

650
00:41:09,309 --> 00:41:14,809
♪ Geomungo · 6-string zither (National Gugak
Center recording)

651
00:41:12,271 --> 00:41:18,233
Down, down, into the deep blue, where the
sunlight gives up.

652
00:41:18,483 --> 00:41:23,202
Against his shell, Steady could feel the
rabbit's heart.

653
00:41:23,222 --> 00:41:24,938
Beating fast.

654
00:41:24,958 --> 00:41:26,986
So fast.

655
00:41:27,176 --> 00:41:31,938
He swam a little slower, and held a little
steadier.

656
00:41:31,958 --> 00:41:35,185
He did not let him fall.

657
00:41:36,335 --> 00:41:49,685
Chapter 9: Before the Dragon Throne
♪ Daechwita — the royal procession  ·
National Gugak Center
Conch horns, long trumpets, drums and shawm:
the music that marched before a Korean king.

658
00:41:50,385 --> 00:41:51,826
Horns blew.

659
00:41:50,535 --> 00:41:57,955
[Culture note] Daechwita is Korea's royal
marching music: horns, drums and a shawm
before the king.

660
00:41:51,846 --> 00:41:54,332
Drums boomed.

661
00:41:54,352 --> 00:41:58,472
Rows of crab soldiers raised their spears.

662
00:41:58,932 --> 00:42:00,119
A parade?

663
00:42:00,139 --> 00:42:01,380
For me?

664
00:42:01,400 --> 00:42:05,189
Steady, you didn't say there'd be a parade!

665
00:42:05,649 --> 00:42:09,933
Welcome, welcome, honored guest!

666
00:42:09,953 --> 00:42:11,379
Come in!

667
00:42:11,399 --> 00:42:12,826
Come in!

668
00:42:12,846 --> 00:42:15,082
Mind the door!

669
00:42:15,482 --> 00:42:20,390
Steady walked beside his friend and said
nothing at all.

670
00:42:21,990 --> 00:42:25,058
So. This is a rabbit.

671
00:42:25,078 --> 00:42:29,310
Lord Terrapin, is this the one?

672
00:42:29,710 --> 00:42:31,764
Yes, Majesty.

673
00:42:32,374 --> 00:42:33,839
Your Majesty!

674
00:42:33,859 --> 00:42:36,253
Clover of Clover Hill.

675
00:42:36,273 --> 00:42:37,614
Delighted.

676
00:42:37,634 --> 00:42:40,926
Now, about my seat of honor-

677
00:42:41,326 --> 00:42:43,593
Seize him!

678
00:42:44,143 --> 00:42:47,639
Crab claws snapped shut.

679
00:42:44,673 --> 00:42:50,173
♪ Geomungo · 6-string zither (National Gugak
Center recording)

680
00:42:47,679 --> 00:42:50,412
Kelp ropes wound tight.

681
00:42:50,452 --> 00:42:55,192
In one breath, the honored guest was a
prisoner.

682
00:42:55,712 --> 00:42:57,336
What is this?

683
00:42:57,356 --> 00:42:58,637
What did I do?

684
00:42:59,097 --> 00:43:03,966
The cure for my sickness is inside that
rabbit.

685
00:43:03,986 --> 00:43:07,180
Doctor, you may begin.

686
00:43:07,580 --> 00:43:10,948
Hold still, little one.

687
00:43:10,988 --> 00:43:13,765
It's only your liver.

688
00:43:13,805 --> 00:43:19,197
The King cannot wait.

689
00:43:19,717 --> 00:43:21,110
Steady.

690
00:43:21,330 --> 00:43:23,738
Look at me.

691
00:43:23,958 --> 00:43:28,479
You said we were friends.

692
00:43:29,179 --> 00:43:33,716
Yes. The moment you saw at the very start.

693
00:43:33,846 --> 00:43:37,765
Steady could not say a single word.

694
00:43:38,905 --> 00:43:42,866
But the fastest thing about a rabbit is not
his feet.

695
00:43:42,906 --> 00:43:44,883
It's his mouth.

696
00:43:45,403 --> 00:43:48,004
Ha! Ha ha ha!

697
00:43:48,024 --> 00:43:51,444
Oh, Majesty.

698
00:43:51,464 --> 00:43:54,814
You poor, poor king.

699
00:43:55,274 --> 00:43:58,097
Why is it laughing?

700
00:43:58,557 --> 00:44:00,497
My liver?

701
00:44:00,517 --> 00:44:01,965
It's not in here.

702
00:44:01,985 --> 00:44:03,917
I left it at home.

703
00:44:14,947 --> 00:44:16,747
♪ Oh, Majesty, dear Majesty, I'd give it
with a smile,

704
00:44:16,747 --> 00:44:18,547
♪ A rabbit's liver cures a king, it's
famous, mile on mile!

705
00:44:18,547 --> 00:44:20,447
♪ But every rabbit knows a thing your doctor
never read,

706
00:44:20,447 --> 00:44:22,247
♪ We never keep our livers in!

707
00:44:22,247 --> 00:44:24,747
♪ (He never keeps it in?)

708
00:44:25,847 --> 00:44:29,447
♪ One small thing, Your Majesty, one small
thing,

709
00:44:29,447 --> 00:44:33,147
♪ It's hanging on a pine by a mountain
spring!

710
00:44:33,147 --> 00:44:35,647
♪ (A pine by a spring? A pine by a spring!)

711
00:44:36,747 --> 00:44:38,547
♪ Each morning when the sun comes up, we
take our livers out,

712
00:44:38,547 --> 00:44:40,447
♪ We wash them in the silver stream and
swish them all about,

713
00:44:40,447 --> 00:44:42,247
♪ We hang them on a pine tree branch to dry
in the fresh air,

714
00:44:42,247 --> 00:44:44,047
♪ And that's where mine is swinging now!

715
00:44:44,047 --> 00:44:45,847
♪ (It's where?)

716
00:44:45,847 --> 00:44:47,647
♪ Up there!

717
00:44:47,647 --> 00:44:49,547
♪ If only your good terrapin had told me
what you need,

718
00:44:49,547 --> 00:44:51,347
♪ I would have packed it with my lunch and
brought it here at speed!

719
00:44:51,347 --> 00:44:54,947
♪ Is this the truth, you rabbit?

720
00:44:54,947 --> 00:44:58,657
♪ Upon my ears, it's true!

721
00:45:09,547 --> 00:45:12,047
♪ (Can a rabbit? Could a rabbit? Would a
rabbit? Should a rabbit?)

722
00:45:13,147 --> 00:45:14,947
♪ (Eolssigu!)

723
00:45:14,947 --> 00:45:18,547
♪ One small thing, Your Majesty, one small
thing,

724
00:45:18,547 --> 00:45:22,247
♪ It's hanging on a pine by a mountain
spring!

725
00:45:22,247 --> 00:45:24,747
♪ (A pine by a spring? A pine by a spring!)

726
00:45:25,847 --> 00:45:29,547
♪ So send me home, Your Majesty, I'll be
back by noon!

727
00:45:29,547 --> 00:45:31,347
♪ (With the liver?)

728
00:45:31,347 --> 00:45:35,347
♪ With the liver! And a spoon!

729
00:45:47,247 --> 00:45:49,266
Doctor!

730
00:45:49,306 --> 00:45:54,019
Can a rabbit really take out its own liver?

731
00:45:54,539 --> 00:45:57,024
Hmm. Hmm-hmm.

732
00:45:57,044 --> 00:45:59,740
The book doesn't say it can't.

733
00:46:00,140 --> 00:46:02,410
Lord Terrapin.

734
00:46:02,430 --> 00:46:04,576
You traveled with him.

735
00:46:04,596 --> 00:46:09,181
You are the one creature here who never
lies.

736
00:46:09,311 --> 00:46:11,295
Is it true?

737
00:46:11,695 --> 00:46:14,729
Clover looked at Steady.

738
00:46:14,769 --> 00:46:17,127
Steady looked at his king.

739
00:46:17,377 --> 00:46:21,883
He felt that rabbit's heart beating on his
shell,

740
00:46:21,883 --> 00:46:24,716
all the way across the sea.

741
00:46:25,876 --> 00:46:28,221
It is true, Majesty.

742
00:46:28,621 --> 00:46:32,932
It was the first lie Steady had ever told.

743
00:46:33,542 --> 00:46:35,624
Then go!

744
00:46:35,694 --> 00:46:37,071
Both of you!

745
00:46:37,141 --> 00:46:41,361
Go back at once and fetch that liver!

746
00:46:41,911 --> 00:46:42,756
Phew.

747
00:46:43,906 --> 00:46:53,156
Chapter 10: Back to the Shore
♪ Pansori · Sugungga — 'The Rabbit's
Revenge'  ·  National Gugak Center
Safe on land, the rabbit jeers: "Listen,
you! Hear what you really are!"

748
00:46:53,856 --> 00:46:57,924
All the way back, neither of them said a
word.

749
00:46:54,056 --> 00:46:59,556
♪ Haegeum & piri · Korean fiddle and reed pipe
(National Gugak Center recording)

750
00:46:58,054 --> 00:47:00,039
No whale waved.

751
00:47:01,639 --> 00:47:04,527
But the moment his feet touched sand,

752
00:47:04,527 --> 00:47:07,920
Clover leaped off and ran up the beach!

753
00:47:08,200 --> 00:47:15,785
[Culture note] In the old pansori, the
rabbit now mocks the terrapin at length.
It's a crowd favorite.

754
00:47:08,320 --> 00:47:11,541
Ha! A liver on a pine tree!

755
00:47:11,561 --> 00:47:14,482
Who takes out his own liver?

756
00:47:14,502 --> 00:47:17,222
You fish-brained fools!

757
00:47:27,312 --> 00:47:30,762
♪ Silly old terrapin, slow old shell,

758
00:47:30,812 --> 00:47:33,662
♪ Who takes out a liver? Couldn't you tell?

759
00:47:33,712 --> 00:47:37,362
♪ It's in my tummy where it's always been,

760
00:47:37,412 --> 00:47:40,962
♪ Right where a liver is meant to be in!

761
00:47:41,012 --> 00:47:44,862
♪ Catch me if you can, catch me if you can,

762
00:47:44,912 --> 00:47:50,212
♪ Hop, hop, hop to the mountain, catch me if
you can!

763
00:47:50,412 --> 00:47:52,312
♪ (Eolssigu!)

764
00:47:53,212 --> 00:47:56,452
♪ Tell your king with his fishy crown

765
00:47:56,502 --> 00:47:59,962
♪ To hang his liver on a pine upside down,

766
00:48:00,012 --> 00:48:03,612
♪ And tell the doctor with his whiskers
grey,

767
00:48:03,662 --> 00:48:07,262
♪ Next time read the whole book through,
okay?

768
00:48:07,312 --> 00:48:10,962
♪ A rabbit's mouth is faster than a rabbit's
feet,

769
00:48:11,012 --> 00:48:15,162
♪ And a rabbit's feet are faster than
anything you'll meet!

770
00:48:15,212 --> 00:48:19,162
♪ Catch me if you can, catch me if you can,

771
00:48:19,212 --> 00:48:24,412
♪ Hop, hop, hop to the mountain, catch me if
you can!

772
00:48:24,712 --> 00:48:26,612
♪ (Eolssigu!)

773
00:48:33,212 --> 00:48:37,262
♪ Catch me if you can, catch me if you can,

774
00:48:37,312 --> 00:48:41,962
♪ Hop, hop, hop to the mountain, catch me if
you can!

775
00:48:42,012 --> 00:48:43,162
♪ (Eolssigu!)

776
00:48:43,212 --> 00:48:45,802
♪ So long, slowpoke! Ha, ha, ha!

777
00:48:49,822 --> 00:48:52,857
So long, you slow old rock!

778
00:48:52,877 --> 00:48:56,088
Tell your king to try seaweed!

779
00:48:56,488 --> 00:48:57,574
I know.

780
00:48:57,974 --> 00:48:58,955
You know?

781
00:48:58,975 --> 00:49:00,318
You know what?

782
00:49:00,778 --> 00:49:04,909
I carried you across the whole sea.

783
00:49:04,929 --> 00:49:08,886
Your heart was beating on my back the entire
way.

784
00:49:09,076 --> 00:49:13,679
And a liver sits right next to the heart.

785
00:49:13,699 --> 00:49:16,141
You had it with you all along.

786
00:49:16,541 --> 00:49:19,917
Then why did you tell your king it was true?

787
00:49:20,437 --> 00:49:24,097
Because you said we were friends.

788
00:49:24,557 --> 00:49:30,500
For once in his life, the fastest mouth on
the mountain had nothing to say.

789
00:49:31,110 --> 00:49:32,911
You lied.

790
00:49:33,131 --> 00:49:35,492
To your own king.

791
00:49:35,712 --> 00:49:37,233
For me.

792
00:49:37,933 --> 00:49:39,893
First time in my life.

793
00:49:39,913 --> 00:49:43,170
It tasted like old seaweed.

794
00:49:43,570 --> 00:49:45,207
Then don't go back!

795
00:49:45,247 --> 00:49:46,852
Stay here!

796
00:49:46,892 --> 00:49:48,756
Clover Hill has room.

797
00:49:48,796 --> 00:49:51,183
I'll share the seat of honor!

798
00:49:51,703 --> 00:49:54,552
My king is still sick.

799
00:49:54,572 --> 00:49:57,843
And my feet belong in the sea.

800
00:49:57,863 --> 00:50:00,452
I have to tell him the truth.

801
00:50:00,852 --> 00:50:03,132
They'll punish you!

802
00:50:03,352 --> 00:50:06,871
That doctor has a knife!

803
00:50:07,571 --> 00:50:08,929
Probably.

804
00:50:08,949 --> 00:50:11,150
Goodbye, Clover.

805
00:50:11,170 --> 00:50:13,891
Hold on to your hill.

806
00:50:14,351 --> 00:50:18,253
And the terrapin slid into the waves.

807
00:50:18,383 --> 00:50:19,838
Slowly.

808
00:50:19,968 --> 00:50:21,288
Of course.

809
00:50:38,188 --> 00:50:42,138
♪ The water's cold on the long way down,

810
00:50:42,188 --> 00:50:45,838
♪ The light goes out where the kelp beds
drown,

811
00:50:45,888 --> 00:50:49,538
♪ I told one lie, and it saved a friend,

812
00:50:49,588 --> 00:50:54,178
♪ I'll tell the truth, and I'll pay in the
end.

813
00:50:55,888 --> 00:50:59,938
♪ A heavy shell, a heavy shell,

814
00:50:59,988 --> 00:51:03,538
♪ I carried a friend and I carried him well,

815
00:51:03,588 --> 00:51:07,238
♪ Now all I carry is what I must tell,

816
00:51:07,288 --> 00:51:10,098
♪ A heavy, heavy shell.

817
00:51:10,588 --> 00:51:14,138
♪ He called me a rock, he called me slow,

818
00:51:14,188 --> 00:51:17,838
♪ He's right about that, and I let him go,

819
00:51:17,888 --> 00:51:22,138
♪ He's up on his hill where the clover
grows,

820
00:51:22,188 --> 00:51:26,778
♪ And that's where a rabbit belongs, I know.

821
00:51:27,488 --> 00:51:31,238
♪ Steady, steady, one flipper at a time,

822
00:51:31,288 --> 00:51:35,768
♪ Slow as the tide, but I'll get there fine.

823
00:51:40,788 --> 00:51:43,738
♪ My King once opened a net for me,

824
00:51:43,788 --> 00:51:47,138
♪ He said, "Go on, little one, swim free."

825
00:51:47,188 --> 00:51:50,138
♪ If I must answer with my shell,

826
00:51:50,188 --> 00:51:54,228
♪ Let the truth ring out like a bell.

827
00:51:58,088 --> 00:52:01,838
♪ A heavy shell, a heavy shell,

828
00:52:01,888 --> 00:52:05,538
♪ I carried a friend and I carried him well,

829
00:52:05,588 --> 00:52:09,238
♪ Now all I carry is what I must tell,

830
00:52:09,288 --> 00:52:12,098
♪ A heavy, heavy shell.

831
00:52:26,268 --> 00:52:43,068
Chapter 11: The Rabbit Who Ran the Other Way
♪ Geomungo sanjo — one long, low phrase  ·
National Gugak Center
The six-string zither, plucked with a bamboo
stick: deep and slow, like a heart making up
its mind.

832
00:52:43,768 --> 00:52:47,547
That night, Clover was home.

833
00:52:43,968 --> 00:52:49,468
♪ Haegeum & piri · Korean fiddle and reed pipe
(National Gugak Center recording)

834
00:52:47,567 --> 00:52:49,884
Safe on his hill.

835
00:52:49,904 --> 00:52:53,744
Sitting on the seat of honor, all alone.

836
00:52:54,204 --> 00:52:56,545
You got away, boy!

837
00:52:56,565 --> 00:52:58,432
From a king!

838
00:52:58,452 --> 00:53:01,918
Nobody on this mountain ever did that!

839
00:53:02,318 --> 00:53:06,584
We'll tell the story for a hundred years.

840
00:53:06,604 --> 00:53:08,182
Well, I will.

841
00:53:08,202 --> 00:53:10,318
I'm the eldest.

842
00:53:10,718 --> 00:53:11,786
Mm.

843
00:53:12,396 --> 00:53:14,432
You're not sleeping.

844
00:53:14,452 --> 00:53:19,083
You're not even eating clover.

845
00:53:19,103 --> 00:53:21,216
What's wrong with you?

846
00:53:21,616 --> 00:53:24,977
One ear is always up, you know.

847
00:53:25,107 --> 00:53:29,443
But tonight both ears are pointing at the
sea.

848
00:53:30,053 --> 00:53:34,873
Then Clover got up, went to the roots of the
old shade tree,

849
00:53:34,873 --> 00:53:36,589
and began to dig.

850
00:53:36,929 --> 00:53:44,514
[Culture note] Wild mountain ginseng was
worth more than gold in old Korea. Finding
one was a miracle.

851
00:53:37,049 --> 00:53:39,274
Wild mountain ginseng.

852
00:53:39,294 --> 00:53:41,549
I've saved it my whole life.

853
00:53:41,569 --> 00:53:44,747
For the day I got too old to run.

854
00:53:44,937 --> 00:53:47,246
A rabbit runs away.

855
00:53:47,286 --> 00:53:48,864
That's what we do.

856
00:53:48,904 --> 00:53:50,418
Not today.

857
00:54:02,668 --> 00:54:05,668
♪ All my life I ran from the hawk,

858
00:54:05,668 --> 00:54:08,668
♪ All my life I ran from the fox,

859
00:54:08,668 --> 00:54:11,668
♪ Every rabbit knows the rule:

860
00:54:11,668 --> 00:54:14,668
♪ When there's trouble, run like a fool!

861
00:54:14,668 --> 00:54:17,668
♪ But today I'll run the other way,

862
00:54:17,668 --> 00:54:20,668
♪ Run the other way, run the other way!

863
00:54:20,668 --> 00:54:23,668
♪ Down to the sea where my friend has gone,

864
00:54:23,668 --> 00:54:26,668
♪ Run the other way till the break of dawn!

865
00:54:26,668 --> 00:54:29,168
♪ (Go on! Go on!)

866
00:54:29,668 --> 00:54:32,668
♪ Where are you going?

867
00:54:32,668 --> 00:54:35,668
♪ That way's the sea!

868
00:54:35,668 --> 00:54:38,668
♪ Rabbits can't swim!

869
00:54:38,668 --> 00:54:41,668
♪ Well, don't tell me!

870
00:54:41,668 --> 00:54:44,668
♪ I've got a root that I saved for years,

871
00:54:44,668 --> 00:54:47,668
♪ For the day I'm old with droopy ears,

872
00:54:47,668 --> 00:54:50,668
♪ But what's a treasure you never share?

873
00:54:50,668 --> 00:54:53,668
♪ A root in the ground, and nobody there.

874
00:54:53,668 --> 00:54:56,668
♪ Four little leaves on a clover hill,

875
00:54:56,668 --> 00:54:59,668
♪ Happy where my feet stand still.

876
00:54:59,668 --> 00:55:02,668
♪ But my feet won't stay, my feet won't
stay,

877
00:55:02,668 --> 00:55:05,668
♪ Till they carry me to him today!

878
00:55:05,668 --> 00:55:08,168
♪ (Eolssigu!)

879
00:55:08,668 --> 00:55:11,668
♪ But today I'll run the other way,

880
00:55:11,668 --> 00:55:14,668
♪ Run the other way, run the other way!

881
00:55:14,668 --> 00:55:17,668
♪ Down to the sea where my friend has gone,

882
00:55:17,668 --> 00:55:20,668
♪ Run the other way till the break of dawn!

883
00:55:20,668 --> 00:55:23,168
♪ (Go on! Go on!)

884
00:55:23,668 --> 00:55:25,688
♪ Go on!

885
00:55:35,878 --> 00:55:41,805
At dawn, a rabbit stood at the edge of the
sea, holding a root.

886
00:55:42,325 --> 00:55:44,196
I can't swim.

887
00:55:44,236 --> 00:55:46,107
I can't swim.

888
00:55:46,147 --> 00:55:50,308
I really, truly cannot swim.

889
00:55:50,558 --> 00:55:53,144
Hold on to my shell.

890
00:55:53,184 --> 00:55:55,771
Hold on to my shell.

891
00:55:56,291 --> 00:55:58,251
And he jumped!

892
00:55:58,531 --> 00:55:59,982
Splash.

893
00:56:00,002 --> 00:56:02,302
Then bubbles.

894
00:56:02,322 --> 00:56:10,630
Then, very slowly, something round came up
under him.

895
00:56:11,030 --> 00:56:12,547
Clover?!

896
00:56:12,567 --> 00:56:14,737
You can't swim!

897
00:56:15,197 --> 00:56:16,852
I noticed!

898
00:56:16,982 --> 00:56:19,265
And you're still here?

899
00:56:19,285 --> 00:56:21,806
You left yesterday!

900
00:56:22,206 --> 00:56:24,719
I'm a terrapin.

901
00:56:24,739 --> 00:56:28,125
I've gone about as far as a good sneeze.

902
00:56:28,255 --> 00:56:30,386
Why did you come?

903
00:56:30,426 --> 00:56:32,568
They'll have the knife ready.

904
00:56:33,088 --> 00:56:35,429
Because you lied for me.

905
00:56:35,449 --> 00:56:38,569
Now somebody's going to tell the truth for
you.

906
00:56:39,029 --> 00:56:42,541
So the terrapin swam down again.

907
00:56:42,561 --> 00:56:45,284
With his friend on his back.

908
00:56:45,304 --> 00:56:46,662
And a root.

909
00:56:46,682 --> 00:56:49,032
And no plan at all.

910
00:56:50,182 --> 00:56:58,582
Chapter 12: The Cure
♪ Court music · Sujecheon  ·  National Gugak
Center
Royal court music whose name means "a life as
long as the sky." A wish for the King.

911
00:56:59,282 --> 00:57:04,901
Two days later, the Dragon King could hardly
lift his head.

912
00:57:04,941 --> 00:57:07,800
The whole court waited.

913
00:57:08,320 --> 00:57:09,870
They're back!

914
00:57:09,890 --> 00:57:11,844
Both of them!

915
00:57:11,864 --> 00:57:14,063
Did you bring it?

916
00:57:14,083 --> 00:57:16,868
The liver on the pine tree?

917
00:57:17,148 --> 00:57:24,678
[Culture note] A deep bow to the floor, the
jeol, is how Koreans show the greatest
respect or regret.

918
00:57:17,268 --> 00:57:21,709
Steady bowed down until his nose touched the
cold floor.

919
00:57:22,229 --> 00:57:23,665
Majesty.

920
00:57:23,685 --> 00:57:25,777
I lied to you.

921
00:57:25,797 --> 00:57:29,967
The rabbit's liver was never on land.

922
00:57:29,987 --> 00:57:32,549
It was in him all along.

923
00:57:32,679 --> 00:57:34,333
I let him go.

924
00:57:34,463 --> 00:57:37,784
Punish me, not him.

925
00:57:38,394 --> 00:57:40,232
Treason!

926
00:57:40,782 --> 00:57:43,550
And also very rude!

927
00:57:43,950 --> 00:57:49,688
You. The one creature in my sea who never
lied.

928
00:57:50,088 --> 00:57:51,368
Majesty!

929
00:57:51,408 --> 00:57:55,113
If you want a liver, you'll have to go
through me first!

930
00:57:55,363 --> 00:57:59,336
Which, fair enough, is exactly where it is.

931
00:57:59,466 --> 00:58:01,587
But I brought you something better.

932
00:58:01,607 --> 00:58:03,833
Wild mountain ginseng.

933
00:58:03,853 --> 00:58:05,875
My whole life's treasure.

934
00:58:06,335 --> 00:58:08,575
Ginseng?

935
00:58:08,595 --> 00:58:10,379
Wait. Wait.

936
00:58:10,569 --> 00:58:12,670
Page two.

937
00:58:12,690 --> 00:58:15,877
Wild mountain ginseng.

938
00:58:15,897 --> 00:58:22,766
Even better than a liver, actually.

939
00:58:23,166 --> 00:58:25,367
page two?!

940
00:58:25,917 --> 00:58:34,728
Nobody ever reads page two, Majesty.

941
00:58:35,128 --> 00:58:36,509
Rabbit.

942
00:58:35,658 --> 00:58:41,158
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

943
00:58:36,529 --> 00:58:38,870
You were free.

944
00:58:38,890 --> 00:58:40,642
Why come back?

945
00:58:41,042 --> 00:58:43,858
Because he came back for me first.

946
00:58:44,318 --> 00:58:47,522
And you, Lord Terrapin.

947
00:58:47,652 --> 00:58:51,147
Why did you lie to your king?

948
00:58:51,757 --> 00:58:53,998
Because of you, Majesty.

949
00:58:54,018 --> 00:58:57,909
When I was caught in a net, you tore it
open.

950
00:58:58,099 --> 00:59:01,028
You didn't ask what I was worth.

951
00:59:01,048 --> 00:59:04,791
You said: go on, little one, swim.

952
00:59:04,811 --> 00:59:07,473
I only did what you taught me.

953
00:59:07,933 --> 00:59:13,395
The Dragon King said nothing for a long,
long time.

954
00:59:14,005 --> 00:59:16,754
I forgot that prince.

955
00:59:16,974 --> 00:59:24,005
I ate, and shouted, and forgot who I was.

956
00:59:24,435 --> 00:59:30,420
A servant who obeys me is loyal to my words.

957
00:59:30,440 --> 00:59:34,343
A servant who stops me is loyal to me.

958
00:59:34,803 --> 00:59:36,835
Take the ginseng, Majesty.

959
00:59:36,855 --> 00:59:38,709
Less feasting.

960
00:59:38,729 --> 00:59:40,530
More walking.

961
00:59:40,660 --> 00:59:43,403
And once a day, go up and feel the sun.

962
00:59:43,423 --> 00:59:45,736
The cure is where your feet are.

963
00:59:45,756 --> 00:59:46,998
Use them.

964
00:59:47,458 --> 00:59:49,339
Kings don't walk.

965
00:59:49,359 --> 00:59:50,656
Kings are-

966
00:59:51,056 --> 00:59:54,775
Every eye in the hall looked at him.

967
00:59:55,175 --> 00:59:57,636
Kings will learn.

968
01:00:09,526 --> 01:00:14,976
♪ I had a thousand ministers who told me I
was right,

969
01:00:15,026 --> 01:00:20,476
♪ They filled my bowl, they bowed so low,
they fed me day and night,

970
01:00:20,526 --> 01:00:23,376
♪ But one small voice from the back of the
hall

971
01:00:23,426 --> 01:00:26,476
♪ Told me the truth, and it saved us all.

972
01:00:26,526 --> 01:00:31,976
♪ A friend tells you true, a friend tells
you true,

973
01:00:32,026 --> 01:00:35,076
♪ Even when the truth is the hardest thing
to do,

974
01:00:35,126 --> 01:00:37,976
♪ A friend tells you true.

975
01:00:38,026 --> 01:00:41,956
♪ I kept my word, and I broke my word,

976
01:00:43,326 --> 01:00:48,026
♪ The strangest thing that you ever heard,

977
01:00:48,526 --> 01:00:51,376
♪ But a promise to a friend and a promise to
a king

978
01:00:51,426 --> 01:00:54,476
♪ Can turn out to be the very same thing.

979
01:00:54,526 --> 01:00:57,476
♪ I ran away, but I came right back,

980
01:00:57,526 --> 01:01:00,476
♪ Down through the blue on a terrapin's
track,

981
01:01:00,526 --> 01:01:03,276
♪ And I found out, as I held on tight,

982
01:01:03,326 --> 01:01:07,676
♪ A friend is the one who holds on through
the night.

983
01:01:07,726 --> 01:01:11,316
♪ We should have read page two!

984
01:01:13,296 --> 01:01:16,506
♪ I should have gone myself!

985
01:01:17,826 --> 01:01:21,386
♪ (Oh no, you shouldn't!)

986
01:01:22,226 --> 01:01:23,606
♪ (Jota!)

987
01:01:27,726 --> 01:01:31,546
♪ The cure is where your feet are,

988
01:01:35,426 --> 01:01:41,266
♪ Right here, right where you stand.

989
01:01:45,026 --> 01:01:50,576
♪ A friend tells you true, a friend tells
you true,

990
01:01:50,626 --> 01:01:53,876
♪ Even when the truth is the hardest thing
to do,

991
01:01:53,926 --> 01:01:57,076
♪ A friend tells you true.

992
01:02:02,336 --> 01:02:14,286
Chapter 13: Where Your Feet Are
♪ Folk song · 'Kwaejina Chingching Nane'  ·
National Gugak Center
An old victory and harvest chant. The crowd
answers every line: "Kwaejina chingching
nane!"

993
01:02:14,986 --> 01:02:19,787
One month later, a very strange thing was
seen on the beach.

994
01:02:19,807 --> 01:02:21,712
A dragon.

995
01:02:21,732 --> 01:02:22,802
Walking.

996
01:02:23,262 --> 01:02:25,530
Twenty steps!

997
01:02:25,550 --> 01:02:28,066
Count properly, Flounder!

998
01:02:28,466 --> 01:02:30,383
Twenty-one, Majesty.

999
01:02:30,403 --> 01:02:35,113
And please, wait for your ministers.

1000
01:02:35,513 --> 01:02:38,762
His pulse is strong.

1001
01:02:38,782 --> 01:02:41,355
His belly is smaller.

1002
01:02:41,375 --> 01:02:45,896
And he laughs every day.

1003
01:02:46,356 --> 01:02:50,092
The sun on my face.

1004
01:02:50,112 --> 01:02:54,067
Why did nobody tell me about this?

1005
01:02:54,527 --> 01:02:57,534
We did, Majesty.

1006
01:02:57,554 --> 01:03:00,395
For forty days.

1007
01:03:01,785 --> 01:03:03,258
Steady.

1008
01:03:01,985 --> 01:03:07,485
♪ Gayageum · 12-string zither (National
Gugak Center recording)

1009
01:03:03,278 --> 01:03:08,690
From today you are my Ambassador to the
Land.

1010
01:03:08,710 --> 01:03:11,508
The only one I trust.

1011
01:03:11,908 --> 01:03:14,214
Me, Majesty?

1012
01:03:14,234 --> 01:03:17,877
I'm the slowest creature in the sea.

1013
01:03:18,337 --> 01:03:20,077
Exactly.

1014
01:03:20,097 --> 01:03:23,151
You always get there.

1015
01:03:23,171 --> 01:03:26,682
And you tell me the truth when you do.

1016
01:03:27,142 --> 01:03:29,156
Lord Ambassador!

1017
01:03:29,176 --> 01:03:31,029
Can I still ride on your back?

1018
01:03:31,429 --> 01:03:34,609
Hold on to my shell.

1019
01:03:35,069 --> 01:03:39,047
Then a shadow fell across the sand.

1020
01:03:39,087 --> 01:03:44,466
Down from the mountain came, of all things,
a tiger.

1021
01:03:44,986 --> 01:03:47,341
Relax, everyone!

1022
01:03:47,361 --> 01:03:51,522
I'm only here for the party!

1023
01:03:51,652 --> 01:03:56,436
And I'm standing way over here.

1024
01:03:56,476 --> 01:03:58,872
Far from him.

1025
01:03:59,392 --> 01:04:02,121
Steady opened his mouth.

1026
01:04:02,141 --> 01:04:03,928
Just a little.

1027
01:04:04,328 --> 01:04:05,371
Eep!

1028
01:04:06,821 --> 01:04:11,328
That night, land and sea threw a feast on
the beach.

1029
01:04:06,971 --> 01:04:14,556
[Culture note] On Korea's southern coast,
women once danced in a circle under the full
moon all night.

1030
01:04:11,348 --> 01:04:13,454
A small feast.

1031
01:04:13,474 --> 01:04:15,838
The King insisted.

1032
01:04:16,298 --> 01:04:18,699
Just one rice cake.

1033
01:04:18,719 --> 01:04:21,132
Maybe two.

1034
01:04:21,532 --> 01:04:23,413
Majesty!

1035
01:04:23,813 --> 01:04:25,116
One!

1036
01:04:25,576 --> 01:04:31,750
Up on a rock, a terrapin and a rabbit watched
the moon come up out of the sea.

1037
01:04:32,210 --> 01:04:34,523
So, Lord Ambassador.

1038
01:04:34,543 --> 01:04:36,312
Where's home now?

1039
01:04:36,332 --> 01:04:38,446
The sea, or the land?

1040
01:04:38,846 --> 01:04:41,101
Wherever my feet are.

1041
01:04:41,121 --> 01:04:43,242
And wherever you are.

1042
01:04:43,702 --> 01:04:47,517
That's the corniest thing a rock ever said.

1043
01:04:47,977 --> 01:04:50,035
Is it true, though?

1044
01:04:50,435 --> 01:04:53,397
Yes. It's true.

1045
01:04:54,847 --> 01:04:56,816
So there it is.

1046
01:04:56,836 --> 01:04:59,170
The Dragon King's cure.

1047
01:04:59,190 --> 01:05:03,588
A walk, a root, and a friend who told the
truth.

1048
01:05:03,778 --> 01:05:05,954
Now, everybody up.

1049
01:05:05,974 --> 01:05:09,504
One last song, and you know the words by
now!

1050
01:05:20,534 --> 01:05:26,434
♪ Hold on to my shell, hold on to my shell,

1051
01:05:27,934 --> 01:05:34,234
♪ I won't let you fall, I won't let you
fall.

1052
01:05:37,734 --> 01:05:38,334
♪ (Jota!)

1053
01:05:38,334 --> 01:05:41,884
♪ The cure is where your feet are,

1054
01:05:41,934 --> 01:05:45,974
♪ Right here, right where you stand.

1055
01:05:47,094 --> 01:05:51,614
♪ (Eolssigu!)

1056
01:05:55,934 --> 01:06:00,634
♪ I ran my whole life, now I'm standing
still!

1057
01:06:01,734 --> 01:06:05,984
♪ I'm slow, but I got there, and I always
will!

1058
01:06:06,034 --> 01:06:09,484
♪ I walk every morning, I eat just one cake!

1059
01:06:09,534 --> 01:06:13,984
♪ I'm King of the Mountain, with a bandaged
nose, for goodness' sake!

1060
01:06:14,034 --> 01:06:18,484
♪ We passed the buck, now we're passing the
drum,

1061
01:06:18,534 --> 01:06:22,124
♪ Everybody sing, here we come!

1062
01:06:23,334 --> 01:06:27,594
♪ Steady, steady, one flipper at a time,

1063
01:06:27,734 --> 01:06:32,214
♪ Slow as the tide, but I'll get there fine.

1064
01:06:32,434 --> 01:06:36,694
♪ Four little leaves on a clover hill,

1065
01:06:36,934 --> 01:06:40,864
♪ Happy where my feet stand still.

1066
01:06:43,234 --> 01:06:44,614
♪ (Jota!)

1067
01:06:45,534 --> 01:06:48,284
♪ The cure is where your feet are,

1068
01:06:48,334 --> 01:06:50,284
♪ Right here, right where you stand.

1069
01:06:50,334 --> 01:06:52,484
♪ The cure is where your feet are,

1070
01:06:52,534 --> 01:06:55,264
♪ Right here, right where you stand.

1071
01:06:55,314 --> 01:06:59,874
♪ (Eolssigu!)

1072
01:07:09,634 --> 01:07:11,234
♪ (Jota!)

1073
01:07:11,334 --> 01:07:17,334
♪ (Hold on to my shell, hold on to my
shell…)

1074
01:07:18,734 --> 01:07:21,434
♪ (I won't let you fall…)

1075
01:07:30,264 --> 01:07:33,742
Go find your own clover hill.

1076
01:07:33,762 --> 01:07:40,363
And if a turtle ever asks you to go for a
swim, ask to see page two!
